# What kinds of thing exist, and what may be said about them.
#
# This file is compiled into the extraction schema: class names become the
# entity types the model may use, object properties become the relation types,
# and every property's rdfs:domain / rdfs:range becomes a triple constraint that
# extraction is validated against. Nothing outside this file can be extracted.
#
# Four things the compiler requires, each of which has cost somebody a run:
#
#   * Declare classes with `a owl:Class`. `a rdfs:Class` alone compiles to
#     nothing at all and the pack extracts no entities.
#   * Declare relations with `a owl:ObjectProperty`, and give every one of them
#     BOTH rdfs:domain and rdfs:range. A relation missing its range is paired
#     with every entity type in the ontology; `packs validate` rejects that.
#   * Names arrive upper-cased. `tpl:written_by` is `WRITTEN_BY` everywhere
#     downstream — in resolve.yaml, eval.yaml, retrieval.yaml and the graph.
#   * A class and a property may not share a name. Both reach the model as label
#     vocabularies, and a shared name makes the extracted type ambiguous.
#
# Keep it small. Every type you declare is a type the model will try to use, and
# a type nothing resolves is a type that adds noise to every measurement. Three
# classes and three relations is a real pack; twenty of each is a wish list.

@prefix tpl:  <http://example.org/template/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#################################################################
#    Classes
#################################################################

tpl:Book a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Book" ;
    rdfs:comment "A published book. Also a document: its description is what the corpus ingests." .

tpl:Author a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Author" ;
    rdfs:comment "A person credited with writing a book." .

tpl:Publisher a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Publisher" ;
    rdfs:comment "The imprint that published a book." .

#################################################################
#    Object properties — the relations extraction may claim
#################################################################

# The relation this pack scores. It is deliberately the one the structured
# source already states, so gold needs no annotation: the metadata says who
# wrote each book, and extraction is measured on whether it read the same thing
# out of the prose.
tpl:written_by a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "written by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The book was written by this author." ;
    rdfs:domain tpl:Book ;
    rdfs:range  tpl:Author .

tpl:published_by a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "published by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The book was published by this imprint." ;
    rdfs:domain tpl:Book ;
    rdfs:range  tpl:Publisher .

# A relation between two of the same type is fine, and is the shape the
# `backbone_edges` gold generator wants. Note what that costs: to score it, one
# document has to mention *both* ends, which is rarer than it sounds.
tpl:influenced_by a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "influenced by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The author's work draws on this other author's." ;
    rdfs:domain tpl:Author ;
    rdfs:range  tpl:Author .

#################################################################
#    Datatype properties — what may be said about one entity
#################################################################

# These become the property lists the extractor is given. Some providers cannot
# carry them at all — Google's Developer API rejects the schema outright — so
# GraphPack disables them per provider rather than per pack. See ENGINE.md.

tpl:title a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "title" ;
    rdfs:domain tpl:Book ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:string .

tpl:year a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "year" ;
    rdfs:domain tpl:Book ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:string .

tpl:name a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "name" ;
    rdfs:domain tpl:Author ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:string .
